  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\n====== Pleasant Weather with Cool Mornings and Warm Afternoons =====\n\nIt is a clear, cool morning with temperatures ranging from the upper \n30s in decoupled valleys to around 50 degrees on ridges. Most places \nwill see temperatures bottom out in the upper 30s and lower 40s this \nmorning. Winds are calm - high pressure at the surface continues to \ninfluence our weather. River valley fog has just started to develop \nin portions of central KY, and we'll likely see fog expand at least \nalong the Kentucky and Cumberland rivers through sunrise. Patchy fog \nwill be possible elsewhere, most likely near rivers and in typical \nfog-prone locations. \n\nBroad mid and upper level ridging remains in place over the central \nCONUS today. There is a weak mid-level shortwave disturbance \ngenerating some mid-level clouds early this morning across portions \nof KS and western MO, but this feature is forecast to dive southeast \nover the Lower MS Valley later today. We'll enjoy sunny skies with \nafternoon highs in the low to mid 70s. Based on yesterday's observed \nhigh temps and model 1000-850 mb thickness trends, decided to go \nslightly warmer than the NBM for highs today. The only other short \nterm change was to again lower afternoon dewpoints, as afternoon \nmixing to near 4 kft should bring some drier air aloft to the \nsurface. This will likely produce afternoon RH values in the 25-35 \npercent range, though a light NE wind will keep the fire danger low. \n\nThe forecast heading into the weekend is largely unchanged. Upstream \nridging aloft, combined with sfc high pressure centered off to our \nN/NE, will preserve the dry airmass and give us a stretch of \nspectacular weather. Expect several dry days with fairly steady \ntemps as well (chilly early mornings and pleasant afternoons). \n\nThe upper level ridge slowly drifts east with time and finally moves \noverhead this weekend. Model consensus suggests the ridge will \ndeamplify through the early to middle portion of next week, with an \nactive pattern emerging across the north-central US. The vast \nmajority (>90%) of ensemble members keep us dry through next \nTuesday, however. Forecast confidence is much lower beyond Tuesday \ndue to increasing model spread.",
